            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the cross product of 3-D vectors: a third vector at right angles to both.\n\nIts direction is perpendicular to the plane the two inputs lie in, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span - largest when they are at right angles, 0 when they are parallel. Order matters: in 1 x in 2 points the opposite way from in 2 x in 1 (the right-hand rule: fingers from in 1 towards in 2, thumb along the result).\n\nReach for it to find a surface normal from two edges of a triangle, the axis to turn about to get from one direction to another, which way is \"up\" given a forward and a sideways direction, or a torque from a lever arm and a force.\n\nIt works on whole stacks at once: 24 bone directions crossed with 24 others (both 24 x 3), or 24 directions crossed with one vector, give 24 answers in one step. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cross <dim>\nDefault: dim -1 - the vectors run along the last dim.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector, or a stack of them (a list is turned into a tensor). Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector or stack, held: send it first. Its shape must match in 1, or broadcast against it (one vector goes with every row).\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the 3 numbers of each vector run along: -1 (the default) for N x 3 stacks, 0 for 3 x N.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncross product:\nThe perpendicular vectors, the shape of the inputs together. Vectors that are not 3 long are refused, with a note in verbose mode.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree vectors - x, y and z, one per row - each crossed with z (held). x times z is (0 -1 0): minus y. y times z is (1 0 0): x. z times z is 0 0 0: parallel vectors have no perpendicular.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dot measures how much two vectors agree; the cross product is what they do not share. t.linalg.norm gives its length (the area). np.cross is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the cross product of 3-D vectors: a third vector at right angles to both.\n\nIts direction is perpendicular to the plane the two inputs lie in, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span - largest when they are at right angles, 0 when they are parallel. Order matters: in 1 x in 2 points the opposite way from in 2 x in 1 (the right-hand rule: fingers from in 1 towards in 2, thumb along the result).\n\nReach for it to find a surface normal from two edges of a triangle, the axis to turn about to get from one direction to another, which way is \"up\" given a forward and a sideways direction, or a torque from a lever arm and a force.\n\nIt works on whole stacks at once: 24 bone directions crossed with 24 others (both 24 x 3), or 24 directions crossed with one vector, give 24 answers in one step. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cross <dim>\nDefault: dim -1 - the vectors run along the last dim.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector, or a stack of them (a list is turned into a tensor). Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector or stack, held: send it first. Its shape must match in 1, or broadcast against it (one vector goes with every row).\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the 3 numbers of each vector run along: -1 (the default) for N x 3 stacks, 0 for 3 x N.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncross product:\nThe perpendicular vectors, the shape of the inputs together. Vectors that are not 3 long are refused, with a note in verbose mode.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree vectors - x, y and z, one per row - each crossed with z (held). x times z is (0 -1 0): minus y. y times z is (1 0 0): x. z times z is 0 0 0: parallel vectors have no perpendicular.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dot measures how much two vectors agree; the cross product is what they do not share. t.linalg.norm gives its length (the area). np.cross is the numpy version."
        },
